☸️ Kubernetes Deployments

Kubernetes Deployments — understanding how Kubernetes manages application availability, scaling, updates, and health.

Today I explored the complete relationship between Deployments, ReplicaSets, Pods, and Containers, and how these components work together to maintain the desired state of an application.

Key concepts covered:

🔹 Deployment architecture and YAML configuration
🔹 spec, replicas, selector, and template
🔹 ReplicaSets and their role in maintaining Pod replicas
🔹 Kubernetes self-healing by automatically replacing failed/deleted Pods
🔹 Scaling Deployments up and down
🔹 Rolling Updates and deployment strategies
🔹 Rollout management using kubectl rollout
🔹 Pausing and resuming rollouts
🔹 maxUnavailable and maxSurge
🔹 Readiness and Liveness Probes for application health
🔹 Monitoring and managing Deployments and ReplicaSets using kubectl

One of the most useful parts was seeing these concepts in action — especially how Kubernetes automatically maintains the desired number of Pods and performs rolling updates without bringing the entire application down.

Another step forward in understanding how Kubernetes manages applications reliably in a production environment. 🚀

